{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall bring an offering made by fire to HaShem seven days.</b> To make known that the intermediate days of the festival (חול המועד) are not entirely profane, for since they include an additional communal offering beyond the daily offering (תמיד), just as on a day of holy convocation, they are undoubtedly included within the festival. He did not specify the details of the additional offerings (מוספין) here, because that is not the present intention, just as he did not specify them for the other festivals either. In this same manner he wrote regarding the festival of Sukkot, to indicate the sanctity of the intermediate days. Regarding Rosh HaShanah, however, he also mentioned <b>an offering made by fire to HaShem</b> in order to indicate that there is an additional offering beyond that of the new moon, as it says: מִלְּבַד עֹלַת הַחֹדֶשׁ וּמְנַחֲתָהּ (Numbers 29:6). But for the Sabbath, the festival of Weeks (Shavuot), and the Day of Atonement (Yom Kippur), he did not mention the additional offerings at all, for what he wrote regarding Yom Kippur — <b>and you shall bring an offering made by fire to HaShem</b> — refers to the burnt offerings and sin offerings of the High Priest and the congregation, which are explained in the section אַחֲרֵי מוֹת. He included the Sabbath, Pesach, and the festival of Matzot in a single utterance, because all three were commanded before the giving of the Torah. But for the other festivals he devoted a separate utterance to each. He began the utterance for the festival of Weeks from the omer, because from then begins the harvest of the omer and the counting of the weeks, which pertain to the festival called ״חַג קָצִיר״ (festival of harvest) and ״חַג שָׁבוּעוֹת״ (festival of weeks), on which thanks are given to the blessed El. For indeed among the intentions of the festivals are prayer and thanksgiving, as in the festival of the month of spring: prayer to El for the spring, and thanksgiving for the redemption. And since the success of the harvest depends on the temperament of the season from the beginning of spring until the harvest, as it says: שָׁבֻעוֹת חֻקּוֹת קָצִיר יִשְׁמָר לָנוּ (Jeremiah 5:24), the omer was a thanksgiving for the spring, like one who brings first fruits of the field to its owners, and the offering accompanying it was a prayer for the future, and the counting was a daily reminder for prayer, and the festival of harvest was a thanksgiving for the goodness of the harvest, and the festival of ingathering (חג האסיף) for the goodness of the ingathering."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:37:34.019184609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}